your hands help tell your life story  from the calluses of an expert bass  player to the healed burns of a seasoned  chef hands can also help unveil the  lives of those far before our time our  cousins the Neanderthals who went  extinct over 40,000 years ago over the  past decades we've seen increasing  complexity in the remnants of  Neanderthal lifestyle such as small  stone tools although it was clear from  their Anatomy that Neanderthal hands  could form a precise pincer like grip to  make tools interpretations of their  fossils have suggest that they usually  used a different more power oriented  grip new methods to analyze ancient  bones have reconciled our cultural  understanding of Neanderthal behavior  with their fossilized remains the  impressive leg muscles of an avid  cyclist or massive forearms on a drummer  aren't preserved in fossils but the  attachments of these hard working  muscles leave scars behind marks that  can tell us about the work they did  typically the greater the size of these  marks called n thesese the stronger the  muscle attached to the bone a previous  study of 19th century human bones found  a close relationship between life  occupation and n thesese tailors or  painters those that perform delicate  precise tasks displayed a pattern of  entha sees different from bricklayers or  carpenters that engaged in more intense  manual labour these patterns reflect two  categories of hand grips precision grips  and power grip in precision grips the  thumb and index fingers do the heavy  lifting while power grips rely more on  the thumb and pinky researchers used a  3d scanner to image early modern humans  and Neanderthals fossils then determine  their hand grip through rigorous  statistical analysis  all the Neanderthal fossils were in the  precision grip category which supports  archaeological evidence of Neanderthals  fashioning small stone tools a power  grip would have been too strong to  create some of these delicately flake  tools in contrast half the early modern  human samples from around 40,000 years  ago displayed precision gripping while  the other half displayed power gripping  in the future these methods can be used  to provide insight about the hand grip  of other early humans as well as how  these ancestors used other muscles what  answers do your hands hold  [Music]
 

